// THE SOCKET IS A FAMILY FACT AND NOW HAS ONE HOME. Every Altra and Altra Max part is LGA4926,
// and the package mechanicals are stated family-wide by the platform specification and the
// datasheet's mechanical section rather than per SKU. Until now each catalog row minted its own
// CpuSocket literal and extdeps.cpu.ampere_altra_package reached through the M128-30 row — an
// ALTRA MAX part — to obtain the socket for a module named for Altra.
//
// That is the §3 fork in its quiet form: nothing was wrong with the VALUE, since both parts really
// do share the socket, so no witness could fail. What was wrong is that a family fact had no
// authority, and one product's row was silently standing in as one. The cost lands the first time
// a Max-specific fact rides that reference, or the first time someone edits the Max row believing
// it governs only Max.
//
// The platform owner rides on the family row for the same reason the rest of it does: which
// platform an LGA4926 socket belongs to is a family fact, not a per-SKU one, and a contact count
// alone does not identify a socket.
// THE MOUNT DESIGNATION IS THE ONE THE DATASHEETS ATTEST, not a name minted here. ILM4926 is named
// by two Ampere datasheets and the OCP Mt. Jade specification, and extdeps.cpu_attachment.ilm4926
// carries that attestation and the open question of which physical part it denotes. For the cooling
// question that ambiguity does not bite: whatever ILM4926 turns out to name, it is the retention
// interface a cooler bolts to, which is exactly the fact a mount identity is for.
data ampere_lga4926_cooling_mount: ProcessorCoolingMount = ProcessorCoolingMount {
  platform_owner: ampere_vendor,
  designation: "ILM4926",
}

data ampere_lga4926_socket: CpuSocket = CpuSocket {
  platform_owner: ampere_vendor,
  termination: SocketedLandGrid,
  contact_count: 4926,
  cooling_mount: ampere_lga4926_cooling_mount,
  figure_standing: CitedToAuthority { authority: ampere_altra_max_rev_a1_datasheet_authority },
}

